</mj-text>
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin-top: 2px !important;">
    Murdaugh gets a retrial
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="Murdaugh gets a retrial"
  href="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>What happened?</strong> Alex Murdaugh’s murder convictions and sentence for killing his wife, Maggie, and son, Paul, were overturned by the South Carolina Supreme Court. Justices found that court clerk <a href="https://apnews.com/article/murdaugh-killings-appeal-overturned-65a2ea0610bdb80763b39838ab4fcdb6" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none;
                border-bottom: 2px solid #4d3060;
                color: black;">Becky Hill improperly influenced jurors</a>. The court said Hill suggested Murdaugh was guilty, attacked his credibility, and denied him a fair hearing. Prosecutors said they plan to retry Murdaugh, possibly in 2026.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Details:</strong> Jurors said Hill told them to watch Murdaugh’s body language and not be fooled or confused by his defense. The justices ruled that her comments pushed jurors toward finding him guilty and damaged his right to a fair hearing. The court also said Hill appeared motivated by a book trade linked to her hearing memoir. Hill has since pleaded guilty to lying about her conduct during the case.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Monetary crime:</strong> Prosecutors may use minimal evidence to explain why Murdaugh may have sought to divert attention from his collapsing monetary and theft schemes. However, the justices warned that graphic or emotional details about the victims he stole from could unfairly influence jurors. Some of those victims were disabled, vulnerable, or in hardship, which could make jurors dislike Murdaugh for reasons unrelated to the murder charges.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>

