    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin-top: 2px !important;">
    Warsh takes charge
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="Warsh takes charge"
  href="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>What happened? </strong>Kevin Warsh was narrowly confirmed by the Senate as the <a href="https://edition.cnn.com/2026/05/13/economy/kevin-warsh-confirmation-trump-fed-chair" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none;
                border-bottom: 2px solid #4d3060;
                color: black;">17th chair of the Federal Reserve</a>, replacing Jerome Powell after an eight-year tenure. Warsh triumphed by a 54-45 vote, mostly along party lines, making it the most partisan Fed chair confirmation in history. His appointment comes as inflation rises, partly due to the US-Israel war with Iran, complicating Trump’s push for cuts.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>What to expect? </strong>Warsh is likely to push for a leaner, less interventionist Federal Reserve. He has signaled plans to shrink the Fed’s $6.7T balance sheet. He may also coordinate more closely with the Treasury and could reduce policy meetings from eight a year to as few as four. Warsh is also expected to hold fewer press conferences, cut the Washington-based workforce, and give fewer hints about future interests.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Background: </strong>Kevin Warsh is a former Wall Street banker, attorney, and Federal Reserve governor. He studied public policy at Stanford University and law at Harvard before working in investment banking at Morgan Stanley. He later served as an economic adviser in George W. Bush’s White House and joined the Fed’s Board of Governors in 2006, becoming the youngest person ever confirmed to the board at 35. Warsh served through the global monetary crisis until 2011, gaining a reputation as a market-focused policymaker and inflation hawk.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-section
